A||||4||GarethWright||At what temperature should my v6 run?||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||09-28-2001||01:57 AM||||When I first got my 87 gt, after warming the engine up and driving around, the temperature guage would sit right on the large bar midway between 100 and 220.  But now it seems to heat up a lot faster and when on the highway for more than 15 mins, im up at 220, and unless I turn off AC and get off the highway fast it keeps climbing with a nasty hot-something smell.  The radiator fan runs all the time the ignition is on since I bought it, and it runs so that air is pulled through the radiator to under the hood, which I think is right.  so, what temp is Right for my car, and is there any problems with running with no thermostat (constantly circulating through radiator should keep temps down right?).  Thanks in advance<BR>-Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000001||btoth||09-28-2001||09:53 AM||||Well, durring the summer, my GT would run at about 220 on a warm day.  I have a 195 deg. thermostat. (I think.)  This week has been really cool out and I have a hard time getting it above 160 (half way between the 100 and 220).<P>If you haven't done anything to the car and your coolent level is fine... maybe it's just the themostat gone bad.  The symptoms you describe remind me of when I filled my coolant wrong and I had an air bubble in it or something and the temp would keep rising.<P>Running without the thermostat shouldn't hurt to test to see if that's the problem, but I think it messes with your computer somehow. (I'm not an expert, sorry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 09-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rick 88||09-28-2001||10:54 AM||||Time to inspect your whole system. Replace your old thermostat with a Stant 180 degree unit instead of the 195. Check and replace any faulty radiator caps. Make sure nothing has gotten wedged in front of the radiator. Inspect all your hoses and replace any that look worn. Check for loose clamps on the rest. Test your coolant for a 50/50 mix. Flush and fill the system if it has been over two years following the factory fill procedure.<P>Your cooling fan should only run if the A/C is on, or the temp hits 220. You can also install a low temp fan switch to turn on the fan sooner without having the A/C on. <P>I'm not sure about Fieros, but running some vehicles with no thermostat in traffic will cause overheating. The coolant circulates too fast. At least this is true in our hot climate. I almost forgot. Check to see the black plastic air dam under the nose is not missing or badly damaged. Without this, the car could overheat as well.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||09-28-2001||01:23 PM||||Everything you said is right Rick88. You covered all the bases.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000004||Voytek||09-28-2001||02:49 PM||||Rick - that gives ME an idea on my car.  It seems to take quite a long time to warm up.  When it starts running hot, it takes a long time to cool off.  Perhaps the thermostat opens too soon and stays open.  Thanks, I'll try that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
